      "worked": "Reflecting in both axes gives $g(x)=-f(-x)=-2^{-x}-a$. At an intersection,\n$$\\begin{align*}2^x+a&=-2^{-x}-a\\\\2^x+2^{-x}&=-2a.\\end{align*}$$\nLet $t=2^x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}t+\\dfrac1t&=-2a\\\\t^2+2at+1&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThe roots have product $1$, so two distinct real roots have the same sign. Exactly one positive root requires a repeated positive root. Set the discriminant equal to zero:\n$$\\begin{align*}4a^2-4&=0\\\\a^2&=1\\\\a&=\\pm1.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $a=1$, the repeated root is $t=-1$, which is inadmissible. For $a=-1$, the repeated root is $t=1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}2^x&=1\\\\x&=0\\\\y&=2^0-1\\\\y&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $a=-1$ and the unique intersection is $(0,0)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
